# SETBUF(3S)

## NAME

setbuf, setvbuf, setbuffer, setlinebuf - assign buffering to a stream

## SYNOPSIS

```c
#include <stdio.h>

void setbuf(FILE *stream, char *buf);

int setvbuf(FILE *stream, char *buf, int mode, size_t size);

void setbuffer(FILE *stream, char *buf, int size);

int setlinebuf(FILE *stream);
```

## DESCRIPTION

*Setbuf* is used after a stream has been opened but before it is read or written. It causes the
character array *buf* to be used instead of an automatically allocated buffer. If *buf* is the
constant pointer **NULL,** input/output will be completely unbuffered.

A manifest constant **BUFSIZ** tells how big an array is needed:

char buf[BUFSIZ];

**Note:** BUFSIZ is 3072 here, one disk block in *bytes.* A page that says 512 is a PDP-11
page.

**Note:** *setvbuf* is C11 and had no v7 ancestor. It says the same thing as *setbuf* and two
more besides: the *mode* is _IOFBF (fully buffered), _IOLBF (line buffered) or _IONBF
(unbuffered), and *size* is the size of *buf*, which need not be BUFSIZ. A null *buf* with a
buffered mode makes *setvbuf* allocate one of *size* bytes itself. It returns 0, or a non-zero
value if the mode is not one of the three or the size is zero.

**Note:** *setbuffer* and *setlinebuf* are BSD's and are neither C11 nor v7. *Setbuffer* is
*setbuf* with a size: *buf* need not be BUFSIZ bytes, and a null *buf* - or a *size* of zero or
less - makes the stream unbuffered, where *setvbuf* would instead fail the call. *Setlinebuf*
selects line buffering and nothing else, and always returns 0.

**These three modes are not flag bits,** and in particular _IONBF is not the flag of that name
in v7's <stdio.h>: the modes are 0, 1 and 2, while the flag bits are 04 and 0400. That
collision is why v7's _IONBF flag bit is spelled _IOUNBUF now.

A buffer is normally obtained from malloc(3) upon the first *getc* or putc(3) on the file,
except that the standard error stream *stderr* is normally not buffered.

**Note:** v7 said output streams directed to terminals are not buffered either. Here they are
*line* buffered - *stdout* takes _IOLBF when isatty(3) says so - which costs one system call
per line where v7 spent one per character. See stdio(3) for the mechanism, since the *putc*
macro shows no sign of it.

## SEE ALSO

fopen(3), getc(3), putc(3), malloc(3), stdio(3)

## BESM-6 NOTES

### `buf == NULL`, never `!buf`

A null **char \*** is *not* a zero word here: the cast that makes a pointer fat sets the marker
bit over the zero address, so the word is non-zero while the pointer still compares equal to
NULL. The compiler compares the address part for **==**, which is what makes the first spelling
right and the second unreliable. Every routine here is written the first way.

### setlinebuf keeps the buffer the stream has

It turns the mode bit on rather than saying *setvbuf(stream, NULL, _IOLBF, BUFSIZ),* which
would malloc(3) 3072 bytes even for *stdout* - a stream the buffering code serves from static
storage. A stream with no buffer yet is given one on its first write, so nothing is allocated
ahead of need. 512 words is worth this much care in an address space of 28,672.

### _bufsiz exists for setvbuf

v7 wrote BUFSIZ into the buffering code outright, because *setbuf* was the only way to hand a
buffer over and it promised one of exactly that size. A caller that names its own size needs
the stream to remember it, or the next flush overruns the buffer it was given. One word, and it
makes *setvbuf* real rather than advisory.

